A||||6||CW85T-Type||stupid tranny (blown up clutch)||||1||
Z||000000||CW85T-Type||09-25-2000||07:06 PM||||I got my 85 SE v6 4spd apart this weekend.  Car had what seemed like a broken inputshaft.  Clutch FELT like it engaged fine but car went nowhere.<P>after HOURS of work to get the stinking tranny out I found all thats left of the clutch disk was the metal parts the clutch matieral was riveted to and the rivets.  The flywheel and pressureplate were rainbo colord from the heat, but there was no burning clutch smell when it went out. Wierd.<P>I have never seen a clutch do this before... any ideas what would cause this?<P>I am swapping in a 4cyl 5spd tranny while I have the 4spd out.  <P>All I can say is this is a ton of work!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Hope this all makes sense, I am SOOOOOO tired<BR>Chris Wilson<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-25-2000||07:33 PM||||Sounds allot like a split diaphram spring in the pressure plate. If the throwout bound a little could have same result. Could be it was worn to thin and just fell apart.<P>could also been over reved a few times. You have to watch what speed you down shift at and into what gears. Drop into to low a gear at to high a speed you can spin the clutch to 8-10KRPM real easy. Sometimes it explodes right away like that other times it makes it real weak so it falls apart later. Depends on the clutch involved and how high it was over reved.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||CW85T-Type||09-25-2000||08:17 PM||||out of all the pieces that were left what were not dust, they were all pretty close to the thickness of the new clutch i picked up.  <P>There was less than 15K on this clutch  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Not sure if I over revved it or not, but it was sure wierd pulling out a clutch disk with no material on it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Chris Wilson||||reg||1
Z||000003||dman||09-25-2000||09:28 PM||||Hi Chris. I couldn't help but notice that you are in Garden Grove (I'm in the valley). I happen to have a 4 speed muncie lying around but the shift/select cables are in bad shape (mostly the select cable). Since you're putting in the Isuzu 5 speed (which I think is a nice choice), would you have the 4speed cables available for sale? BTW, will you need a new slave cylinder to match the 5 speed transmission or can you use your old 4 speed slave?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||stsmithgt||09-26-2000||08:53 AM||||Sounds like Theogre might have it right with the high speed down shift then.  That's what I was going to say.  Downshifting at too high of a speed can make a clutch disassemble itself and you end up scooping the clutch up out of the bell housing.  Been there, done that with my brothers Challenger he had.  (I DIDN'T DO IT! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I only fixed it.)  ||||reg||6
Z||000005||CW85T-Type||09-26-2000||12:54 PM||||I will have the shifter cables for sale once I get the 5spd ones swapped in.<P>The slave cylinder <I>looks</I> the same.  I'm hoping it will work.<P>No more high speed downshifts for me! This is too much work  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Chris Wilson<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowCG||10-01-2000||12:25 AM||||I'm pretty sure that the slave cylinder is a smaller diameter than the one for the five speed,my car has one,allowing a little more throw than the original.More effort too,but I like the way the clutch engages at the top of the travel now.BTW,the travel at the tranny is now 1",don't know what it was originally.||||reg||1
